                  <provenance type="#b"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_137">Chester, Cheshire.
                        Franciscan convent</orgName><p>This attribution has a complex evolution
                        within the history of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain, which can be
                        summarized as follows:</p>
                     <p>In the first edition (1941), the attribution was accepted, albeit with a
                        question mark.</p>
                     <p>Conversely, in the second edition (1964), the attribution was rejected.</p>
                     <p>This downgrade is reflected on the corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great
                        Britain index card. The card originally had the following note:
                           <q>Provenance inferred from others in the same collection</q>. However,
                        this note was later crossed out, and the following summative comment added
                        instead: <q>Should be rejected. No evidence</q>.</p>
                     <p>There were no relevant changes in the Supplement (1987). During MLGB3
                        (2010-2015), the attribution was once again accepted (though now without a
                        question mark). The folllowing reason was given: The medieval binding and
                        style of binding furniture is the same on Gray's Inn, MSS 1–9, 11–12, and
                        23, identifiably deriving from the same library. MSS 1, 2, 5, 7, 11, 12
                        contain ex libris inscriptions.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Volumen 7ᵐ (s. xvii) on flyleaf.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The Bostock family of Tattenhall
                     (Cheshire).</provenance>
               </history>
